          <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Industry context</p>
          <h2 class="section-title">Patient safety hinges on infrastructure availability</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose-content">
          <p>Hospitals and clinics depend on electronic health records, imaging systems and connected medical devices that require continuous availability. Network outages can directly impact patient care, making redundancy and rapid failover essential design principles. Ransomware attacks targeting healthcare organisations have intensified, with 2024 seeing strengthened cybersecurity guidelines for protecting protected health information.</p>
          <p>Healthcare data is among the most regulated categories of information. Providers must protect patient privacy, control access to sensitive systems and demonstrate compliance with data protection regulations. The Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) — infusion pumps, patient monitors, imaging equipment — expands the attack surface and requires dedicated security controls. Telehealth adoption has accelerated, requiring low-latency connectivity and HIPAA-equivalent compliance frameworks across the MEA region.</p>
