      <h2 class="section-title mb-12">Sector scenarios & case studies</h2>
      <div class="reveal grid-2">
        <div class="prose-content">
          <p>A regional specialty hospital group in the Gulf faced a critical challenge: its nurse call systems, patient monitoring devices and IP-based surveillance network were running on fragmented infrastructure that could not guarantee the uptime demanded by critical care units. MEAICON designed and deployed a redundant network core with automatic failover across two data paths, ensuring that even a complete link failure would not interrupt clinical communications. The result was 99.99% availability across all connected clinical systems, including telemetry, imaging and electronic health record access.</p>
          <p>In East Africa, a multi-site healthcare provider operating clinics across remote regions struggled with unreliable connectivity that frequently severed telemedicine sessions. MEAICON implemented hybrid WAN links combining fibre and LTE backup, with quality-of-service policies that prioritised telemedicine traffic. Medical staff could conduct uninterrupted remote consultations with specialists in urban centres, and patient data replicated securely to a central repository in near real-time.</p>
          <p>A North African hospital network needed to modernise its surveillance and access control infrastructure while maintaining compliance with national patient-data regulations. MEAICON segmented the security network from clinical traffic, deployed edge recording for surveillance continuity during outages, and implemented role-based access controls. The hospital group could demonstrate audit-ready compliance during inspections while protecting sensitive systems from lateral movement threats.</p>
        </div>
